title: prepIntelligence
---
<b>Siege Preparation — Intelligence</b>: Before the Sultan arrives, strangers still enter his moving camp without attracting much notice. Sphrantzes lays four plans before you. Each cover opens different doors; every agent must live among the men who intend to destroy us.
-> Send a loyal servant disguised as a beggar. +1 agent; +2 days of land-operation delay; +25 camp alert on arrival.
    <<PrepIntelligence 0>>
    Your servant joins the roadside beggars. He spreads a rumour that floods have destroyed a bridge on the army’s route. The scouts will waste two days verifying it; the discrepancy will also make the camp suspicious. Your servant stays behind as an agent.
-> Send a merchant and a prostitute. 2,000 gold; +2 agents and +1 secret. <<if GetInt("iMoney") >= 2000>>
    <<PrepIntelligence 1>>
    The merchant listens over contracts; his companion listens when the officers believe business is finished. Their first reports contain a useful secret.
-> Send a clandestine cell disguised as a performers’ troupe. 5,000 gold; +3 agents. <<if GetInt("iMoney") >= 5000>>
    <<PrepIntelligence 2>>
    A musician, an acrobat and the troupe’s quartermaster follow the army. Their wagons pass between camps, and their audiences include men who would never speak to an imperial envoy.
-> Diplomacy and Spycraft: send a four-person caravan office. 3,000 gold; +4 agents. <<if GetInt("iFocus") == 4 && GetInt("iMoney") >= 3000>>
    <<PrepIntelligence 3>>
    A caravan factor handles contracts, an interpreter carries messages, a farrier works among the horses, and a camp physician visits the wounded. Their professions explain both their movements and their questions.
===
title: prepScorched
---
<b>Siege Preparation — Scorched Earth</b>: Your scouts mark the roads, mills and depots the Ottoman army will use. Every obstacle may buy time. Every burned storehouse also belongs to someone who once expected your protection. Choose one operation. The figures below are the current balance settings.
-> Remove stores and cut crossings. 1,000 gold; delay 2 days; enemy food −3 days; 50 irregulars lost; public morale −3. <<if GetInt("iMoney") >= 1000>>
    <<PrepScorched 0>>
    Our parties evacuate stores and break minor crossings. Ottoman foragers stray into ambushes.
-> Burn depots and harass the march. 3,000 gold; delay 4 days; enemy food −7 days; 150 irregulars lost; public morale −6. <<if GetInt("iMoney") >= 3000>>
    <<PrepScorched 1>>
    Raiding parties strike the depots and wagons. Smoke follows the enemy’s advance, and displaced villagers crowd towards the City.
-> Strip the entire approach. 6,000 gold; delay 6 days; enemy food −12 days; 300 irregulars lost; public morale −9. <<if GetInt("iMoney") >= 6000>>
    <<PrepScorched 2>>
    The army will find ruined mills, empty granaries and contested roads. The soldiers call it preparation; the refugees have other names for it.
-> Fortifications and Artillery: destroy powder and cannon convoys. 4,000 gold; delay 4 days; enemy food −5 days; up to 2 cannons destroyed. <<if GetInt("iFocus") == 0 && GetInt("iMoney") >= 4000>>
    <<PrepScorched 3>>
    Engineers identify the powder wagons and prepare timed charges. The explosion destroys guns that will never reach the walls.
-> Mobilisation: ride out and bring over a Serbian mercenary band. 3,000 gold; requires 50 cavalry. Gain 50 cavalry, 150 archers, 300 infantry; Ottoman Christian allies −500. <<if GetInt("iFocus") == 1 && GetInt("iMoney") >= 3000 && GetInt("iCavalry") >= 50 && GetInt("iBalkans") >= 500>>
    <<PrepScorched 4>>
    Your riders bring an imperial guarantee of pay and safe passage. The Serbian captain changes sides with five hundred men. The same men leave the Ottoman rolls and enter yours.
-> Leave the approaches untouched. We cannot fund the operation.
    The villages keep their stores and crossings. The Ottoman army will find its route unimpeded.

===
title: prepFortress
---
<<SetInt iRumeliLandmark 1>>
<b>A Fortress Rises on the Bosphorus</b>: The report reaches you before the morning bells. Across the water, Mehmed’s men are clearing ground. Stone barges crowd the landing; armed guards turn away questions. Your pilots need no architect to explain the plan: grain ships from the Black Sea could soon pass beneath Ottoman guns. Notaras spreads a chart across the council table. “He plans to choke our access to the Pontus, Emperor. We can buy what we need while the passage remains open. Or we can take more active measures. We are still at peace, at least on paper, but the chess pieces are already moving. We should learn precisely what is being built—and how we might interfere with the Sultan’s plans.”
-> Send a formal complaint to the Sultan. Building a fortress on our doorstep endangers an already fragile peace. 500 gold; one diplomatic secret. <<if GetInt("iMoney") >= 500>>
    <<Change iMoney -500>>
    <<Change iSecrets 1>>
    Your envoys are turned away with smiles that barely conceal a predatory satisfaction. The Sultan’s courtiers know that soon the City will be cut off from the lifeline that feeds it. It is now clear to you that war is inevitable.
    Nevertheless, your envoy’s time at the court has yielded insights that may prove useful later.
-> There is little we can do but make haste. Order as many shipments of food as possible while the straits remain open. 6,000 gold; +3,000 provisions. <<if GetInt("iMoney") >= 6000>>
    <<Change iMoney -6000>>
    <<Change iProvisions 3000>>
    You order Loukas Notaras to arrange for six ships to sail to the Black Sea ports and return laden with grain. Over the following days, he sends instructions to liquidate the imperial assets you still hold there and use the proceeds to purchase supplies. Where necessary, your agents offer merchants twice the price, payable after the war.
    Your promises do not carry equal weight in every port. Some merchants insist on payment now, and the treasury must cover part of the cost. Nevertheless, the ships return with a substantial cargo of grain—a reserve that could make a considerable difference in the coming siege.
-> Armed reconnaissance. Send five of your best Varangians to infiltrate the fortress and discover its weaknesses. 50% return chance; success gives +1 secret and +30 percentage points to later infiltration. <<if GetInt("iVarangians") >= 5>>
    <<set $chance = dice(100)>>
    <<if $chance <= 50>>
    <<RumeliRecon varangians true>>
    You choose the Varangians because they are the most elite soldiers in your service. Under cover of darkness, they scale an unfinished section of wall, enter the works and steal a copy of the plans.
    All five return safely. This intelligence will improve your future operations against the Straitcutter Fortress.
    <<else>>
    <<RumeliRecon varangians false>>
    You choose the Varangians because they are the most elite soldiers in your service. Under cover of darkness, they attempt to scale an unfinished section of wall and enter the works.
    But even elite soldiers sometimes fall prey to chance. Your Varangians are caught. The following morning, your scouts report that all five can be seen impaled upon the fortress’s battlements.
    You will need to find another way to destroy it.
    <<endif>>
-> Send one spy to reconnoitre the works. 75% success; failure loses the agent. Success gives +1 secret and +30 points to later infiltration. <<if GetInt("iSpies") >= 1>>
    <<set $chance = dice(100)>>
    <<if $chance <= 75>>
    <<RumeliRecon spy true>>
    The agent returns among the suppliers with plans concealed in a ledger. One secret gained; the reconnaissance advantage is secured.
    <<else>>
    <<RumeliRecon spy false>>
    The agent is exposed and executed. No report reaches the City.
    <<endif>>
-> Our answer will come from our own walls. No cost; troop morale +5.
    <<Change fTroopRep 0.05>>
    The garrison hears your defiance. The Ottoman work continues.
===
title: prepGrain
---
<b>Siege Preparation — Overseas Grain Missions</b>: Merchant agents can charter their own ships to import grain. This does not change the mission of your military fleet. Payment is immediate; provisions arrive only when the voyage is complete.
-> Charter an Aegean convoy: 4,000 gold; 3,000 provisions in 30 days. <<if GetInt("iMoney") >= 4000>>
    <<PrepGrain 0>>
    The agents depart with sealed contracts.
-> Buy from the Black Sea: 6,000 gold; 5,000 provisions in 45 days. Requires open straits. <<if GetInt("iMoney") >= 6000 && GetBool("bStraitsOpen")>>
    <<PrepGrain 1>>
    The agents sail north before the passage closes.
-> Use Galatan merchants: 3,000 gold; 2,500 provisions in 21 days. <<if GetInt("iMoney") >= 3000 && GetInt("iGalataState") != 3>>
    <<PrepGrain 2>>
    The podesta’s merchants arrange the cargo.
-> Decline the contracts.
    No mission is dispatched.
===
title: prepNaval
---
<b>Siege Preparation — Naval Warfare</b>: The captains ask for ships, the shipwrights for timber, and the pilots for silence. These proposed investments are under development and provide no bonuses yet.
-> Charter three armed merchantmen: 6,000 gold; +3 ships. — Under development <<if false>>
    <<PrepNaval 0>>
    Three armed merchantmen join the fleet.
-> Prepare repair yards: 4,000 gold and 30 materials; recover damaged survivors after up to two fleet reports. — Under development <<if false>>
    <<PrepNaval 1>>
    The yards can restore laid-up damaged hulls. They cannot recover ships that have sunk.
-> Establish naval magazines: 3,000 gold and 500 provisions; first three fleet reports gain 25% effect. — Under development <<if false>>
    <<PrepNaval 2>>
    Dedicated stores let the fleet carry more operational cargo and remain effective longer.
-> Create a hidden coastal anchorage: 4,500 gold and one committed agent; raiding reports gain 50% until discovery, 20% discovery risk per use. — Under development <<if false>>
    <<PrepNaval 3>>
    A remote landing becomes a covert base. The agent remains there; discovery ends its benefit.
-> Train pilots and signal crews: 2,500 gold; first two grain or volunteer reports gain 25% effect. — Under development <<if false>>
    <<PrepNaval 4>>
    The pilots rehearse night approaches and agree signals with the harbour watch.
-> Make do with the fleet we have. — Continue
    The captains return to their ships. No new investment is made.

===
title: prepGalataGovernor
---
<b>Who Shall Govern Galata?</b>: The capture is confirmed. The keys are yours; the settlement is not. A governor serves the Emperor, while an autonomous lord holds power of his own.
-> Keep Galata under an imperial administrator.
    <<GrantGalata imperial>>
    Galata remains under direct imperial control.
-> Appoint Giovanni as imperial governor, without autonomy. <<if GetBool("bCharWarrior0")>>
    <<GrantGalata governor>>
    Giovanni receives an office, not an independent lordship.
-> Promise Galata to Giovanni after victory. Loyalty +15; retain imperial control for now. <<if GetBool("bCharWarrior0")>>
    <<GrantGalata later>>
    The promise is recorded. It will be honoured upon victory if Galata remains in your hands.
-> Grant autonomous rule immediately. Loyalty +30; Galata defences +25. <<if GetBool("bCharWarrior0")>>
    <<GrantGalata now>>
    Giovanni takes responsibility for the district and reinforces its defences.
-> Restore the podesta and friendly neutrality.
    <<GrantGalata neutral>>
    Galata’s independent charter is restored.
===
title: prepPass
---
<b>Preparation council</b>: You reserve no further project this month.
-> Let the month pass.
    The calendar advances without an additional preparation benefit.
===
title: galata26
---
<b>Galata Joins the Emperor</b>: With goodwill at its height, the podesta offers allegiance.
-> Accept imperial control. Galata becomes an Ottoman military target.
    <<GalataState 1>>
    The imperial banner rises beside Galata’s own. The podesta remains administrator; this voluntary accession does not replay the capture-governance event.
-> Preserve their neutrality; receive one diplomatic claim instead.
    <<Change iGalataClaims 1>>
    Their friendship remains a diplomatic asset.
===
title: galata27
---
<b>Galata Distracts the Turks</b>: Galatan intermediaries can send Ottoman officers chasing a false report.
-> Accept the diversion. Ottoman land operations delayed 2 days.
    <<Change iEnemyDelay 2>>
    The distraction buys two days.
-> Keep the contacts in reserve. Gain one diplomatic claim.
    <<Change iGalataClaims 1>>
    The promise of assistance is recorded.
===
title: galata28
---
<b>Galata Sends Troops</b>: Volunteers offer to cross the harbour and join the defence.
-> Welcome 150 infantry and 100 archers.
    <<Change iInfantry 150>>
    <<Change iArchers 100>>
    The volunteers take their places.
-> Ask them to strengthen Galata instead. Defences +15.
    <<Change iGalataDefences 15>>
    The district prepares its own walls.
===
title: galata29
---
<b>Galata Offers a Loan</b>: Merchants offer a ten-year loan. The treasury receives the gold now; no repayment falls due during this siege.
-> Accept 5,000 gold on ten-year terms.
    <<Change iMoney 5000>>
    <<Change iGalataDebt 5000>>
    The ten-year agreement is entered in the imperial accounts. For now, the money can pay for the defence.
-> Decline the loan.
    No obligation is incurred.
===
title: galata30
---
<b>Galata Trades with the Turks</b>: Merchants are supplying the besiegers despite your protests.
-> Pay 2,000 gold to buy out the cargo. Goodwill +5. <<if GetInt("iMoney") >= 2000>>
    <<Change iMoney -2000>>
    <<Change fGalataRep 0.05>>
    The cargo is withdrawn.
-> Allow the trade. Ottoman food +5 days.
    <<EnemyFoodDays 5>>
    The supplies enter the enemy camp.
===
title: galata31
---
<b>Galata Sells War Plans</b>: An intermediary offers the Ottomans plans of the City’s defences.
-> Spend one secret to intercept the transaction. <<if GetInt("iSecrets") >= 1>>
    <<Change iSecrets -1>>
    Your contacts stop the exchange.
-> We cannot stop it. Current wall loses 5 points of protection; camp alert +10.
    <<GalataLeak>>
    The enemy receives useful plans.
===
title: galata32
---
<b>Galata Opens Its Gates to the Turks</b>: At the lowest point of relations, a faction in Galata prepares to admit Ottoman soldiers.
-> Spend 5,000 gold to buy the gate captains. Goodwill +15. <<if GetInt("iMoney") >= 5000>>
    <<Change iMoney -5000>>
    <<Change fGalataRep 0.15>>
    The gates remain closed.
-> We cannot prevent the betrayal. Galata passes to Turkish control.
    <<GalataState 3>>
    Ottoman troops occupy the district.
===
title: giovanniWarning
---
<b>Giovanni Asks for Guarantees</b>: Giovanni’s captains resent your demands on autonomous Galata. He asks for a settlement before resentment becomes defiance.
-> Pay his men 3,000 gold. Loyalty +20. <<if GetInt("iMoney") >= 3000>>
    <<Change iMoney -3000>>
    <<CharacterLoyalty 0 20>>
    <<SetInt iGiovanniWarning 1>>
    His captains accept the settlement.
-> Refuse. Loyalty −10; the warning remains unresolved.
    <<CharacterLoyalty 0 -10>>
    <<SetInt iGiovanniWarning 1>>
    Giovanni leaves with his demands unanswered.
===
title: giovanniThrone
---
<b>The Purple or the Walls</b>: Giovanni holds Galata, commands armed followers and believes you have broken faith with him. Now his supporters demand that he take the imperial throne. This crisis follows an earlier warning.
-> Buy a settlement: 6,000 gold; loyalty +30. <<if GetInt("iMoney") >= 6000>>
    <<Change iMoney -6000>>
    <<CharacterLoyalty 0 30>>
    <<SetInt iGiovanniCrisis 1>>
    His officers accept terms and abandon the challenge.
-> Send 75 Varangians to arrest the conspirators. Lose 25 guards; Giovanni is imprisoned and Galata returns to imperial control. <<if GetInt("iVarangians") >= 75>>
    <<Change iVarangians -25>>
    <<Change bCharWarrior0 FALSE>>
    <<SetInt iCourtState0 4>>
    <<GalataState 1>>
    <<SetInt iGiovanniCrisis 1>>
    The guard breaks the conspiracy.
-> Without the means to resist, concede the throne.
    <<SetInt iGiovanniCrisis 1>>
    <<SetInt iDeposed 1>>
    You are deposed. Your reign ends; Giovanni’s seizure of the throne does not promise that the City will survive.
===

title: cardinalReturns
---
<b>The Cardinal Returns</b>: Cardinal Isidore has returned to Constantinople. His servants carry his baggage through the palace gates, and news of his arrival travels considerably faster.<br><br>A Greek churchman and a cardinal of Rome, Isidore has spent years working towards reconciliation between the churches. He believes the agreement reached at Florence offers a way to restore Christian unity—and secure assistance for the City.<br><br>Gennadios Scholarios remains its most determined opponent. A scholar, monk and former imperial official, he regards the proposed settlement as a surrender of the faith the empire is supposed to defend. He has already asked whether the Cardinal intends to appear before you in Latin dress.<br><br>Before long, you will have to decide how the Union is to be received in Constantinople. For now, there is still time to speak with both men.
-> Go personally to welcome Cardinal Isidore. Isidore loyalty +5; Gennadios loyalty -5.
    <<CardinalReception 0>>
    You meet Isidore before his servants have finished unloading. "Majesty. You honour me." For a moment, the Cardinal appears to forget the formal greeting he had prepared. He takes your offered hand with both of his. Gennadios hears of the reception before the afternoon is out. He makes no public comment.
-> Keep Isidore outside the palace gates until he changes into Orthodox robes. Isidore loyalty -5; Gennadios loyalty +10.
    <<CardinalReception 1>>
    Your instructions reach the Cardinal at the entrance. He listens without interrupting, then asks that his baggage be opened. When he is finally admitted, he wears the robes of a Greek churchman. His greeting is courteous and brief. Gennadios expresses his approval. "It is well that he should remember the Church from which he came."
-> Leave his reception to the palace officials. No effects.
    <<CardinalReception 2>>
    Rooms are prepared, baggage is carried inside, and the Cardinal is informed that you will receive him when your duties permit. By evening, both Isidore and Gennadios are requesting an audience.
===
